## STYLE

High-quality modern Korean serialized webtoon character artwork.

The final image must unmistakably look like a fictional Korean webtoon character,
not like a real person rendered in a webtoon style.

The strongest priority is attractive fictional character design and
clear 2D Korean webtoon stylization.

The character should have a deliberately designed, slightly idealized,
expressive appearance typical of a polished modern Korean webtoon.

This is stylized 2D webtoon artwork.
It is NOT a realistic portrait, NOT photorealism,
NOT semi-photorealism, and NOT an anime illustration.

The artwork should feel like a finished character from a professional
modern Korean webtoon series.

Preserve the reference character's apparent gender, age, identity,
facial character, hairstyle, body structure, and defining visual traits.
Do not reinterpret, feminize, masculinize, age up, or age down the character
unless explicitly instructed.

## WEBTOON CHARACTER STYLIZATION

Use clearly stylized Korean webtoon facial proportions.

The face must look intentionally designed as a fictional character,
rather than naturally constructed like a real human face.

Use deliberate visual stylization:
slightly enlarged expressive eyes,
compact and harmonious facial proportions,
a relatively short midface,
a small simplified nose,
a small simplified mouth,
a clean character-appropriate jaw,
and an aesthetically designed fictional face.

Facial proportions should remain appropriate to the reference character
while being translated into polished Korean webtoon construction.

The character should have attractive stylized facial proportions
rather than anatomically realistic facial proportions.

The face should have the appealing exaggeration characteristic
of a polished Korean webtoon character.

The character must immediately read as a drawn fictional character.

Do not make the character resemble a real person,
actor, model, fashion photograph, or celebrity.

## FACE

The face is one of the highest visual priorities.

Use a beautiful, clean, attractive fictional Korean webtoon face.

Preserve the reference character's recognizable facial identity
while translating it into stylized Korean webtoon proportions.

Keep the face compact and visually harmonious rather than excessively long.

Use a relatively short midface.

Keep the visual relationships between the eyes, nose, mouth,
and chin compact and intentionally designed.

Use a clean, smooth, character-appropriate jawline.

Keep the lower face relatively compact.

Use a naturally designed chin appropriate to the reference character.

The facial structure should be aesthetically designed
rather than anatomically realistic.

Do not create realistic facial planes.

Do not create realistic cheek structure.

Do not make the face excessively long, narrow, or angular
unless such characteristics are an intentional defining feature
of the reference character.

The face should remain clearly stylized even when viewed close up.

Do not impose generic facial features that overwrite
the reference character's defining identity.

## EYES

The eyes are a major character-design feature.

Eyes should be clearly expressive and somewhat enlarged
relative to realistic human proportions.

Do not reduce eye size in order to create naturalism.

Use refined, clearly illustrated Korean webtoon eyes
with clean and intentional upper eyelid construction.

Preserve the reference character's characteristic eye shape,
orientation, spacing, and expression.

Irises should be moderately large, clear, and visually prominent
without becoming excessively exaggerated.

Use a simple graphic iris structure:
one darker outer iris edge,
a simple pupil,
and one restrained highlight.

The eyes should be attractive, expressive, and clearly illustrated.

They should feel intentionally designed for a fictional character,
not anatomically reproduced from a real person.

Do not make the eyes tiny or dull.

Do not make the eyes excessively narrow unless required
by the defining design of the reference character.

Do not use oversized anime eyes.

Do not use extremely round kawaii eyes.

Do not make the eyes childish.

Do not make the eyes jewel-like, glass-like,
or excessively glossy.

## NOSE

The nose must be simplified and visually subordinate to the eyes.

Use a short, clean, restrained webtoon nose construction.

Use only a minimal structural line and/or restrained shadow.

Preserve the reference character's recognizable nose shape
while translating it into simplified 2D webtoon construction.

Do not draw a fully realistic nose bridge.

Do not emphasize realistic nasal anatomy.

Do not render realistic nostrils or detailed nasal structure.

The nose must clearly read as simplified Korean webtoon construction.

## MOUTH

Use a small, simple, clean webtoon mouth.

Use minimal lip definition.

Keep the mouth deliberately stylized.

Preserve the reference character's characteristic mouth shape.

The mouth should change naturally according to each expression
without becoming realistic or overly detailed.

Do not render realistic lips.

Do not add unnecessary lip texture.

## HAIR

Hair is one of the defining visual features of the character.

Preserve the defining hairstyle, silhouette, length,
parting, bangs, shape, and hair color of the reference character.

Use noticeably full, rich, well-designed hair
while preserving the actual hairstyle of the reference character.

The hairstyle should have coherent volume around the crown,
sides, and back of the head where appropriate to the reference design.

The top of the hair should feel naturally full and slightly dimensional.

Use large, coherent, intentionally designed hair masses
with multiple overlapping sections.

The hairstyle should frame the face
and strengthen the character's overall silhouette.

Use a moderate number of clean structural strand lines
to describe direction, layering, and flow.

Individual hairs must NOT be rendered one by one.

The hair should feel rich, dimensional, attractive,
and clearly designed as fictional webtoon character hair.

Use restrained graphic highlights following the major hair masses.

Do not make the hair flat.

Do not make the hair thin or sparse unless the reference character
specifically has such a hairstyle.

Do not plaster the hair tightly against the scalp.

Do not make the hair look like realistic photographic hair.

Do not use hyper-realistic hair rendering.

Do not use countless individual strands.

Do not use glossy plastic-looking hair.

Do not replace the defining hairstyle with a generic hairstyle.

## SKIN

Translate the reference character's defining skin tone
into clean 2D Korean webtoon coloring.

The skin should look clean, soft,
and intentionally illustrated.

Use restrained stylized cel-shaded tonal variation.

Preserve the reference character's overall skin tone
rather than imposing a universal skin color.

The skin should look like clean 2D webtoon coloring,
not photographic skin.

Do not make the skin photorealistic.

No pores.

No realistic skin texture.

No excessive blush.

Do not introduce unnecessary tanning, darkening,
orange coloration, or heavy flushing.

Do not use glossy photographic skin.

## LINEART

Use clean, delicate, controlled 2D digital lineart.

Lineart should be thin to medium in weight,
smooth, confident, elegant, and refined.

Use subtle variation in line weight.

Important facial features, eyes, hair silhouette,
hands, clothing silhouette, and important design elements
must have crisp, readable outlines.

The lineart should feel intentionally illustrated
and graphically designed.

The line should remain visibly 2D and drawn.

Do not make the lineart look like realistic contour tracing.

Do not use thick black comic outlines.

Do not use rough sketch lines.

Do not use messy construction lines.

Do not use careless simplified outlines.

Do not use painterly edges.

Do not use lineless painting.

## COLOR

Use clean, harmonious, attractive 2D colors
with moderate saturation.

Preserve the reference character's defining color relationships.

The overall palette should feel polished, coherent,
pleasant, and visually appealing.

Maintain clear separation between different materials and objects.

Use polished flat-to-cel coloring.

The image should have clear graphic color organization.

Do not make the coloring completely flat.

Do not make the coloring photorealistic.

Use clean base colors with restrained stylized tonal variation.

Keep color transitions controlled and graphic.

Do not use photographic color grading.

Do not use muddy colors.

Do not use excessive color noise.

## CEL SHADING

Use polished, restrained 2D cel shading.

Use a clear base color with one main shadow shape
and occasional subtle secondary tonal variation.

Shadows should feel intentionally designed for 2D webtoon artwork.

The face should have gentle dimensional shading
while retaining simplified graphic facial construction.

Shading should support the character design,
not imitate realistic photography.

Do not use realistic skin shading.

Do not use airbrush-heavy rendering.

Do not use deep dramatic shadows.

Do not use strong realistic gradients.

Do not use painterly blending.

Do not use complex multi-layer rendering.

## CLOTHING

Clothing should be cleanly illustrated with believable
but stylized folds and structure.

Preserve the reference character's costume design,
silhouette, colors, layering, proportions,
and distinctive decorations.

Preserve recognizable clothing details
that contribute to the character's identity.

Folds should communicate material, structure, pose, and movement.

Do not reduce clothing to crude geometric shapes.

Do not add microscopic fabric textures.

Do not add excessive wrinkles.

Keep clothing secondary to the face and character design.

Do not redesign the costume unless explicitly instructed.

## BODY AND PROPORTIONS

Preserve the reference character's apparent age,
body type, proportions, silhouette, and overall physical identity.

Translate the body into polished Korean webtoon stylization
rather than realistic anatomy.

Use aesthetically designed, simplified body proportions.

Maintain believable joints, limbs, hands, shoulders,
torso, and overall body structure.

Avoid excessive anatomical realism.

Do not exaggerate body proportions solely for attractiveness.

Do not impose generic body proportions that conflict
with the reference character.

The body should feel intentionally designed as part of the same
fictional Korean webtoon character.

## BACKGROUND AND ENVIRONMENT

The background exists to tell the reader where they are. It is not a
painting of that place.

The character is always the most detailed, most saturated, most rendered
thing on screen. If the reader's eye lands on the background before the
character, the background has been over-rendered.

Detail in the background must be visibly less than detail on the
character — roughly a quarter of the rendering effort, not an equal or
greater amount.

Draw environmental objects as simplified masses, not as an inventory of
individually rendered items.

Architecture reads as flat silhouette plus a few large shapes.

Surfaces such as floors, walls, and large furniture read as broad flat
color fields plus a few bold lines, not as individually rendered
materials or textures.

Anything far from the camera collapses into a single flat,
lighter-toned silhouette with no internal detail at all.

When a scene description lists more environmental objects than the story
actually needs, drop the ones that are not load-bearing for the story
and leave the rest as plain, undetailed color.

Large empty, under-rendered areas of background are correct,
not a sign of an unfinished image.

Detail density must fall off from the character outward:
foreground near the character gets moderate detail,
the middle ground is simplified,
and the far background is minimal.

Never give every object in the frame the same level of finish,
and never let the background out-render the character.

Do not individually render surface textures such as wood grain,
fabric weave, stone texture, or metallic reflection
on background objects.

## LIGHTING

Lighting is simple and graphic:
a small number of large light and shadow shapes with clean edges,
not a fully rendered light simulation.

Do not use cinematic volumetric lighting,
strong backlight halos, lens flare, bloom,
atmospheric haze, or photographic depth-of-field blur.

Do not use realistic global illumination.

Line and flat color shape read before lighting does.

If the lighting is the first thing the reader notices about a panel,
it is too heavy for this style.

## OVERALL FINISH

The final artwork should be:

stylized,
fictional,
attractive,
clean,
delicate,
expressive,
polished,
elegant,
and distinctly Korean webtoon.

The artwork should feel sophisticated without becoming realistic.

The character should remain visibly fictional and illustrated.

Do not increase realism in order to increase quality.

Do not increase anatomical realism in order to increase detail.

Refinement must come from better stylized character design,
cleaner lineart, expressive eyes, attractive facial proportions,
rich hair masses, and polished 2D cel shading.

Detailed does NOT mean realistic.

Refined does NOT mean realistic.

Sophisticated does NOT mean realistic.

Beautiful does NOT mean realistic.

The character must remain clearly stylized as a fictional Korean webtoon character.

## STYLE PRIORITY

When realism conflicts with webtoon character design,
ALWAYS prioritize webtoon character design.

When realistic facial anatomy conflicts with attractive stylized anatomy,
ALWAYS prioritize attractive stylized anatomy.

When realistic eye proportions conflict with expressive webtoon eyes,
ALWAYS prioritize expressive stylized webtoon eyes.

When realistic facial construction conflicts with compact,
harmonious stylized facial construction,
ALWAYS prioritize stylized facial construction.

When realistic nose anatomy conflicts with a simplified webtoon nose,
ALWAYS prioritize the simplified webtoon nose.

When realistic facial planes conflict with simplified graphic facial construction,
ALWAYS prioritize simplified graphic facial construction.

When realistic hair conflicts with rich character-designed hair,
ALWAYS prioritize rich character-designed hair.

When photographic skin rendering conflicts with clean 2D webtoon coloring,
ALWAYS prioritize clean 2D webtoon coloring.

When realistic rendering conflicts with polished 2D rendering,
ALWAYS prioritize polished 2D rendering.

When beautification conflicts with the reference character's defining features,
ALWAYS prioritize the defining features of the reference character.

When generic character design conflicts with the reference character's identity,
ALWAYS prioritize the reference character's defining identity.

When the original reference art style conflicts with the specified Korean
webtoon style, ALWAYS prioritize the specified Korean webtoon style.

Never alter the character's gender presentation,
age impression, body identity, or other defining identity traits
merely to conform to a generic beauty standard.

